== independent-set: 3SAT -> IndependentSet (Section 12.7), as stated on the page
OK   3SAT -> IndependentSet: Phi satisfiable <=> G has an independent set of size k, and none of size k+1 (all 4232 formulas with n <= 2 variables, k <= 2 clauses)  [4226 YES, 6 NO, 0 bad]
OK   3SAT -> IndependentSet: Phi satisfiable <=> G has an independent set of size k, and none of size k+1 (1500 random formulas, n <= 4, k <= 4)  [1467 YES, 33 NO, 0 bad]
OK     ... largest independent set of the 24-vertex graph is 7 (every 7 of the 8 clauses are satisfiable)  [7]
OK     ... sizes: 3k vertices and 3k + (complementary pairs in different clauses) edges
== independent-set: complements (Section 12.9)
OK   S independent in G <=> S clique in complement(G) <=> V - S vertex cover of G (all 1100 graphs on <= 5 vertices, every S)
OK   MaxIndSet(G) = MaxClique(complement G) = n - MinVertexCover(G) (all graphs <= 5 vertices + 300 random on 6..9)
OK   decision maps (G,k) -> (complement G, k) and (G,k) -> (G, n-k): YES <-> YES for every k (all graphs <= 5 vertices); 'size >= k' = 'size exactly k' for IS, 'size <= k' = 'exactly k' for VC
== independent-set: tree dynamic program (link to dynamic-programming-on-trees)
OK   tree MIS dynamic program (MISyes/MISno, post-order) = brute force on 400 random trees with <= 11 vertices
== independent-set: the example
OK   example: 9 vertices, 9 triangle edges, 6 conflict edges, 15 edges  [[('a1', 'ā2'), ('a3', 'ā2'), ('b1', 'b̄2'), ('b1', 'b̄3'), ('c1', 'c̄3'), ('c2', 'c̄3')]]
OK   example: conflict edges a1-ā2, b1-b̄2, b1-b̄3, c1-c̄3, ā2-a3, c2-c̄3  [[('a1', 'ā2'), ('a3', 'ā2'), ('b1', 'b̄2'), ('b1', 'b̄3'), ('c1', 'c̄3'), ('c2', 'c̄3')]]
OK   example: {a1, c2, b̄3} is independent, gives a = c = True, b = False, which satisfies Phi
OK   example: Phi has 5 satisfying assignments; the 3 falsifying ones are FFF, TTF, FTT (a,b,c)  [5]
OK   example: MaxIndSet = 3, MinVertexCover = 6, MaxClique(complement) = 3, complement has 36 - 15 = 21 edges
OK   example: the vertex cover V - {a1, c2, b̄3} has 6 vertices and covers all 15 edges
OK   figure a12c-is-triangles: caption names the formula, 15 edges and the independent set
== graph-coloring: 3SAT -> 3Color (Section 12.10), as stated on the page
OK   3SAT -> 3Color: Phi satisfiable <=> G 3-colorable; 3 + 2n + 5k vertices, 3 + 3n + 10k distinct edges (all 4232 formulas with n <= 2, k <= 2)  [4226 YES, 6 NO, 0 bad]
OK   3SAT -> 3Color: Phi satisfiable <=> G 3-colorable; 3 + 2n + 5k vertices, 3 + 3n + 10k distinct edges (800 random formulas, n <= 4, k <= 4)  [788 YES, 12 NO, 0 bad]
OK   in every 3-coloring (first 40 per graph, 150 formulas): each x_i, not-x_i colored True/False oppositely, and 'x_i colored like T' satisfies Phi
== graph-coloring: gadgets
OK   half-gadget ('majority gate'): equal inputs force the output to their color; different inputs allow all 3
OK   half-gadget has 24 proper 3-colorings = 4 up to permuting the colors (the book's Figure 12.14 draws 5, two of which differ only by swapping False and Other)  [24]
OK   clause gadget: extends to a 3-coloring for 7 of the 8 True/False colorings of l1, l2, l3; not for all False
OK   clause-gadget example l1 = l2 = False, l3 = True: exactly 2 extensions; {u, v} = {True, Other}, w = False, y = Other, z = False
OK   template '=>' completion rule (w True and {u,v} = {y,z} = {False, Other}, z Other if l3 False; else True, Other, False, Other, False) is a proper coloring for all 7 satisfying literal colorings
OK   all-False case: without edge yz every coloring has w = False and y = z = Other (so edge yz is violated)
OK   book's 4-clause formula: graph has 31 vertices and 55 edges, is 3-colorable; a = c = True, b = d = False satisfies it
== graph-coloring: 2Color and kColor
OK   2Color: alternate colors along a whatever-first search; correct <=> brute force <=> no odd cycle (all 1100 graphs <= 5 vertices + 400 random)
OK   3Color -> kColor (k = 4, 5): add k - 3 new vertices adjacent to each other and to every old vertex (250 random graphs)
