OK     ... and H has exactly sum over satisfying assignments of prod_j (#true literals of c_j) Hamiltonian cycles, so no cycle 'cheats' (40 unsatisfiable formulas, n <= 3, k <= 4)  [0 mismatches]
== hamiltonian-cycle: the book's construction (Section 12.11, 'From 3SAT') is not correct
OK   counterexample formula (all 8 clauses over a, b, c) is unsatisfiable, and a=T, b=c=F falsifies only (ā ∨ b ∨ c), the last clause
OK   book's construction (text: (i,0)..(i,2k)): H has 59 vertices and the Hamiltonian cycle row a left-to-right, (a,16) -> [8] -> (b,16), row b and row c right-to-left, other clauses by ordinary detours  [59 vertices]
OK   book's construction (figure: 2k vertices per row): H has 56 vertices and the Hamiltonian cycle row a left-to-right, (a,16) -> [8] -> (b,16), row b and row c right-to-left, other clauses by ordinary detours  [56 vertices]
OK   book's claim: the variable-gadget graph G alone has exactly 2^n Hamiltonian cycles (n <= 4, k <= 2)
OK   book's construction on (a ∨ c ∨ b) ∧ (b̄ ∨ ā ∨ c) has 'cheating' Hamiltonian cycles: 24 (text version) and 29 (figure version) instead of 16 assignment/literal choices  [(24, 29, 16)]
== hamiltonian-cycle: the example
OK   example: H has 3·8 + 2 = 26 vertices and 6·2·3 + 4·3 + 6·2 = 60 edges  [26, 60]
OK   example: Phi has 6 satisfying assignments; H has exactly 12 Hamiltonian cycles = sum of products  [6, 12, 12]
OK   example: the cycle h1, row a left to right with detour 1 -> [1] -> 2, h2, row b right to left, h3, row c right to left with detour 5 -> [2] -> 4, back to h1, is Hamiltonian; a = T, b = c = F satisfies Phi
OK   example: for a = T, b = c = F, clause 1 has 2 true literals (a, b̄) and clause 2 has 1 (c̄): 2 of the 12 cycles
== hamiltonian-cycle: the book's VertexCover -> DirectedHamCycle
OK   VertexCover -> DirectedHamCycle (book): G has a vertex cover of size k <=> H Hamiltonian, for graphs without isolated vertices and 1 <= k <= n (348 instances)
OK     ... with isolated vertices it can fail: e.g. one edge plus an isolated vertex, k = 3 (cover exists, H has 3 cover vertices but only 2 vertex chains)  [[(1, 0, 1), (2, 0, 1), (2, 0, 2), (3, 0, 1), (3, 0, 2), (3, 0, 3)]]
OK     ... repaired: delete isolated vertices, answer YES if k >= vertices left, else the book's H (361 instances, all graphs <= 4 vertices, 0 <= k <= n)
OK   book's Figure 12.17 graph (edges uv, uw, ux, vx, wx; k = 2): H has 4E + k = 22 vertices and 6E + (2E - V) + 2kV = 52 edges, exactly 2 Hamiltonian cycles; {u, x} (Figure 12.18) is the only cover of size 2  [22 vertices, 52 edges, 2 cycles, covers [{0, 3}]]
